    <string name="goal_definition_link_note">The categories you flag here are the only ones we\'ll ever look at outside your Missions — see Unsupervised Reliability later in setup.</string>
    <string name="goal_definition_continue">Continue</string>

    <!--
        §2.1 Welcome / Product Philosophy. Spec's own framing: "not a screen to sell the
        product, it's a screen to filter for the right user." Three content requirements,
        each with its own string below:
        - welcome_restricts_body: states plainly that Missions restrict phone functionality
        - welcome_tone_body: states that higher tiers include confrontational language by
          design and that it's a choice, not a default — matches FallbackVoiceBank's own
          scoping (Warden Voice used at Operator/Warden/Iron, never Recruit; see that file's
          kdoc), so "a choice, not a default" is accurate to what the app actually does, not
          aspirational copy
        - No urgency/scarcity language anywhere on this screen (explicit anti-pattern,
          Architecture doc §4.1's "deceptive/manipulative design patterns" app-review risk) —
          reviewed against that same behavior-vs-identity-adjacent standard as every other
          user-facing string in this file: describes what the product does, states a
          filtering purpose plainly, never pressures or implies the reader is being tested by
          continuing.
    -->
    <string name="welcome_step_progress">Step 1 of 8</string>
    <string name="welcome_title">Before you start</string>
    <string name="welcome_restricts_body">This app restricts what your phone can do while a Mission is running — apps you\'ve chosen to block are actually blocked, not just discouraged.</string>
    <string name="welcome_tone_body">Higher tiers (Operator, Warden, Iron) give you direct, evidence-based feedback when you break a commitment — that tone is a choice you make by picking a tier, not something every setup gets by default. Recruit stays lower-pressure throughout.</string>
    <string name="welcome_fit_body">If that sounds like more structure than you want right now, that\'s useful to know before you set anything up — you can always start at a lighter tier and move up later.</string>
    <string name="welcome_continue">Continue</string>

    <!--
        §2.3 Tier Explanation — spec's own label: "highest-stakes copy in onboarding."
        Three content requirements per tier, all satisfied per-tier below:
        - enforcement (blocking behavior, countdown mechanics, exit availability) — every
          number here (5s/10s/10s/15s countdown, no-early-dismissal at Warden/Iron, mandatory
          reason entry at Iron) is read directly off InterceptionPolicy.kt's real
          countdownDuration/allowsEarlyDismissal/requiresBreakCommitmentReason functions, not
          invented for this copy — see that file's own kdoc, which cites PRD §14 as the
          literal source for these figures.
        - Voice tone — tier_explanation_operator_voice/warden_voice/iron_voice each quote an
          actual line from FallbackVoiceBank.kt ("You opened a blocked app during an active
          Mission." — that bank's own OFF_ALLOWLIST section), this codebase's own authored
          content, not third-party material. Recruit has no voice line (tier_explanation_
          recruit_voice states this plainly) — InterceptionPolicy.usesWardenVoice(RECRUIT) is
          false, matching PRD §14/§22.1's "Recruit's interception content is informational,
          not Warden Voice at all."
        - casual-exit disclosure — every _exit string below states plainly whether the tier
          allows leaving a Mission before it ends; Warden/Iron's "no casual exit" framing is
          the literal disclosure §2.3 calls "the single most important disclosure in the whole
          flow," so it gets its own dedicated line per tier rather than being folded into the
          enforcement paragraph.

        **Anti-pattern check (§2.3's own explicit requirement) before writing a single line
        below:** no tier's copy states or implies anything about who "needs" it or what kind
        of person picks it — every string describes only what the system does at that tier.
        Recruit is listed first strictly because Onboarding §1's flow diagram and PRD §12.6
        both describe the tiers in Recruit→Iron order, matching tier_selection's own existing
        ordering rationale (see that screen's layout comment) — not because first position is
        meant to read as "the default/easy choice." No tier gets bold/emphasized/differently
        -sized text, a color treatment the others lack, or an icon the others lack; all four
        cards share one identical structural template in the layout, differing only in the
        string content each one points to.
    -->
    <string name="tier_explanation_step_progress">Step 3 of 8</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_title">The four tiers, compared</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_intro">Enforcement gets stricter as you move down this list. Compare all four before choosing — the next screen is where you actually pick.</string>

    <!-- Shared section labels, same three across every card — see layout comment on why a
         shared, identical structural template is how this screen enforces §2.3's
         no-differential-visual-treatment requirement rather than relying on manual upkeep. -->
    <string name="tier_explanation_section_enforcement">Enforcement</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_section_voice">Voice tone</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_section_exit">Leaving a Mission early</string>

    <string name="tier_explanation_recruit_label">Recruit</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_recruit_enforcement">A blocked app during a Mission shows a 5-second informational countdown, then you decide whether to continue the Mission or end it.</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_recruit_voice">No AI feedback line — Recruit\'s interception screen is informational only.</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_recruit_exit">You can end a Mission early at any time, no confirmation required.</string>

    <string name="tier_explanation_operator_label">Operator</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_operator_enforcement">A blocked app shows a 10-second countdown. You can still dismiss it early and continue the Mission if you choose.</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_operator_voice">You\'ll get a direct, evidence-based line describing what happened — e.g. \"You opened a blocked app during an active Mission.\" Never a comment on who you are, only on what you did.</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_operator_exit">You can end a Mission early at any time, no confirmation required.</string>

    <string name="tier_explanation_warden_label">Warden</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_warden_enforcement">A blocked app shows a 10-second countdown with no early dismissal — you have to wait it out before choosing to continue or end the Mission.</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_warden_voice">Same direct, evidence-based feedback as Operator — e.g. \"You opened a blocked app during an active Mission.\" Never a comment on who you are, only on what you did.</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_warden_exit">No casual exit once a Mission starts. Ending it early counts as a violation — there\'s no quiet way to back out mid-Mission.</string>

    <string name="tier_explanation_iron_label">Iron (unlocks after 10 days at Recruit or Operator)</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_iron_enforcement">A blocked app shows a 15-second countdown with no early dismissal. Ending the Mission early requires entering a reason before it\'s confirmed.</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_iron_voice">Same direct, evidence-based feedback as Operator and Warden. Never a comment on who you are, only on what you did.</string>
    <string name="tier_explanation_iron_exit">No casual exit once a Mission starts, same as Warden — plus a mandatory reason entry to confirm ending it early. A separate Crisis Exit control exists for situations that call for immediate relief, logged as a distinct event from a normal early end.</string>

    <string name="tier_explanation_continue">Continue to Tier Selection</string>

    <!--
        §2.6 Core Data Consent — spec's own framing: "required — local storage, Mission
        enforcement," not an optional data use. Two content requirements, each its own
        paragraph below:
        - core_data_consent_required_body: states plainly that local storage is required to
          use the app at all, since Mission enforcement (the app's core function) depends on
          it — matches Architecture doc §3.1's "Mission enforcement must work fully offline,"
          so this paragraph describes a real technical dependency, not a policy choice framed
          as a requirement.
        - core_data_consent_local_first_body: plain-language local-first + optional
          cloud-sync explanation (Architecture doc §3.1 again) — states the on-device
          encrypted store is the source of truth and cloud sync, if it ever exists, would be
          opt-in and additive on top of that, never a replacement for local storage working
          on its own.
        - core_data_consent_delete_body: not one of §2.6's two named requirements, but
          included per Architecture doc §3.2's rescoped-for-personal-use section, which
          singles out "a basic delete-my-data action... still worth having" as the one
          concrete user-facing consequence of that rescoping — mentioning the affordance
          exists (Settings, not this screen — no delete control lives here) is a one-sentence
          honesty cost worth paying while this screen is already talking about what's stored
          locally and why.
    -->
    <string name="core_data_consent_step_progress">Step 6 of 8</string>
    <string name="core_data_consent_title">How your data is stored</string>
    <string name="core_data_consent_required_body">This app stores your Missions, Profiles, and progress in an encrypted database on your device. That storage is required to use the app — Mission enforcement is the core function, and it has to work even when you\'re offline, so there\'s no lighter-weight mode that skips this.</string>
    <string name="core_data_consent_local_first_body">Your device is the source of truth. Nothing is sent anywhere by default. If cloud sync is ever added, it would be an opt-in addition on top of local storage, not a replacement for it.</string>
    <string name="core_data_consent_delete_body">You can wipe your local data at any time from Settings if you decide to stop using the app — that\'s a plain local delete, not a request that has to be sent anywhere or waited on.</string>
    <string name="core_data_consent_continue">Agree and continue</string>

    <!--
        §2.7 Unsupervised Reliability Opt-In — spec's own framing: "separate screen, genuinely
        optional," a real choice with its own value proposition, not core-consent's forced
        agree-to-continue. Four content requirements from the spec, one paragraph each below:
        - unsupervised_reliability_measurement_only_body: measurement only, no enforcement ever
          (PRD §13.3/§13.4 — no scoring pressure, cannot trigger debt/Recovery Mode/Tribunal).
        - unsupervised_reliability_scope_body: scope is limited to categories flagged at Goal
          Definition (§2.2) specifically — not blanket device activity. References that
          screen's own flaggedCategories by name so the link stays visible to the user, same
          reasoning goal_definition_link_note already established in the other direction.
        - unsupervised_reliability_deletable_body: separately deletable at any time, distinct
          from Mission/Score data (Architecture doc §3.2/§3.1's "separately deletable" data
          category framing).
        - unsupervised_reliability_self_report_preview_body: names and previews the monthly
          Brief Self-Control Scale self-report (PRD §13.2.1) up front, rather than surfacing it
          unannounced a month later.
        unsupervised_reliability_intro_body is the value-proposition paragraph §2.7 asks for
        ("why this data helps the user, not just the product") — framed around the co-primary
        KPI reasoning in PRD §5.1 (behavior can revert while capacity holds, or both can
        revert; this signal is what lets the two be told apart), in plain language, not KPI
        jargon.
    -->
    <string name="unsupervised_reliability_step_progress">Step 7 of 8</string>
    <string name="unsupervised_reliability_title">Track your reliability outside Missions?</string>
    <string name="unsupervised_reliability_intro_body">This is optional, and separate from everything else you\'ve agreed to so far. If you turn it on, the app can tell the difference between \"I did fine because a Mission was blocking things\" and \"I did fine on my own\" — which is useful to know about yourself, not just useful to us.</string>
    <string name="unsupervised_reliability_measurement_only_body">Measurement only. This never enforces anything, never blocks anything, and never affects your Discipline Debt, Reputation, Tier, or any consequence path in the app — full stop.</string>
    <string name="unsupervised_reliability_scope_body">It only looks at the categories you flagged on the Goal Definition screen earlier — nothing else on your device is ever included.</string>
    <string name="unsupervised_reliability_deletable_body">This data is stored separately from your Mission history and can be deleted on its own, at any time, without touching anything else.</string>
    <string name="unsupervised_reliability_self_report_preview_body">If you opt in, you\'ll also see a short monthly check-in (the Brief Self-Control Scale, a handful of quick questions) alongside your Monthly Intelligence Report — so it\'s not a surprise the first time it shows up.</string>
    <string name="unsupervised_reliability_switch_label">Enable Unsupervised Reliability tracking</string>
    <string name="unsupervised_reliability_enable_button">Enable and continue</string>
    <string name="unsupervised_reliability_skip_button">Skip for now</string>

